[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No connectivity fault is present between the two Location B hosts. On the access switch (switch10), both access ports are up and participating in the bridge:
- eth1 (to PC15) and eth2 (to PC16) are UP.
- The bridge FDB has learned both host MACs on the expected ports: 86:fc:b9:b1:bd:22 on eth1 (PC15) and 66:51:2c:38:63:0b on eth2 (PC16), confirming bidirectional frame learning/forwarding on the same VLAN.

On the endpoints:
- PC15: 172.18.51.15/24 on eth0, link detected yes.
- PC16: 172.18.51.16/24 on eth0, link detected yes.
- ARP on each host has the peer’s correct MAC (ip neigh shows the expected lladdr).
- Bidirectional ICMP works with 0% loss and sub-millisecond RTTs (pc15→pc16 and pc16→pc15 both succeed). Additional same-subnet hosts (pc17/pc18) can also reach PC15 without loss.

Mechanism: With correct ARP resolution, learned MACs on the correct switch ports, and successful in-subnet pings, frames are being bridged normally; there is no evidence of port down state or filtering on the access switch.

Localization: Switch10 ports eth1 (PC15) and eth2 (PC16); hosts PC15 and PC16.

Root cause: Not a network issue; the reported “can’t reach” condition is not reproducible.

Corrective fix: No changes required on the network. Ask the users to retest; if issues reappear, investigate host firewalls/NIC state or transient host issues, and verify no duplicate IPs on 172.18.51.0/24.
=== END ===